module-whatis {IO::All combines all of the best Perl IO modules into a single nifty object oriented interface to greatly simplify your everyday Perl IO idioms. It exports a single function called io, which returns a new IO::All object. And that object can do it all!}

proc ModulesHelp { } {
    puts stderr {Name   : perl-io-all}
    puts stderr {Version: 0.87}
    puts stderr {Target : x86_64_v3}
    puts stderr {}
    puts stderr {IO::All combines all of the best Perl IO modules into a single nifty}
    puts stderr {object oriented interface to greatly simplify your everyday Perl IO}
    puts stderr {idioms. It exports a single function called io, which returns a new}
    puts stderr {IO::All object. And that object can do it all! The IO::All object is a}
    puts stderr {proxy for IO::File, IO::Dir, IO::Socket, Tie::File, File::Spec,}
    puts stderr {File::Path, File::MimeInfo and File::ReadBackwards; as well as all the}
    puts stderr {DBM and MLDBM modules. You can use most of the methods found in these}
    puts stderr {classes and in IO::Handle (which they inherit from). IO::All adds dozens}
    puts stderr {of other helpful idiomatic methods including file stat and manipulation}
    puts stderr {functions. IO::All is pluggable, and modules like IO::All::LWP and}
    puts stderr {IO::All::Mailto add even more functionality. Optionally, every IO::All}
    puts stderr {object can be tied to itself. This means that you can use most perl IO}
    puts stderr {builtins on it: readline, <>, getc, print, printf, syswrite, sysread,}
    puts stderr {close.}
}

